Given an ellipse C that passes through the point $A(2 \sqrt {6}，2)$, with two foci at $(-2 \sqrt {6}，0)，(2 \sqrt {6}，0)$.
(1) Find the standard equation of ellipse C;
(2) Let line l intersect ellipse C at points A and B, with |AB|=6, find the maximum area of △AOB.

Given an arithmetic sequence $\{a_n\}$, the sum of the first $n$ terms is $S_n$, and it satisfies $a_3+a_5=a_4+8$.  
(I) Find the value of $S_7$;  
(II) If $a_1=2$ and $a_3$, $a_{k+1}$, $S_k$ form a geometric sequence, find the value of the positive integer $k$.

In the figure, $\overline{DC}$ is parallel to $\overline{AB}$. We have $\angle DCA = 50^\circ$ and $\angle ABC = 80^\circ$. Also, $\angle DAC = 30^\circ$. Find $\angle ACB$.

[asy]
pair A,B,C,D;
A = (0,0);
B= (1,0);
D = (-0.1,0.6);
C = (0.8,0.6);
draw((-0.2,0)--(1.2,0));
draw((-0.2,0.6)--(1.2,0.6));
draw(A--C--B);
draw(D--A);
label("$A$",A,S);
label("$B$",B,S);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,N);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
[/asy]

How many ways are there to fill the  $2 \times 2$  grid below with  $0$ 's and  $1$ 's such that no row or column has duplicate entries?

[asy]
	size(2cm);
	draw(unitsquare);
	draw( (0.5,0)--(0.5,1) );
	draw( (0,0.5)--(1,0.5) );
[/asy]

In the diagram below, points $A$, $B$, $C$, and $Q$ are situated so that $QA=3$, $QB=4$, $QC=5$, and $BC=6$. What is the maximum possible area of $\triangle ABC$? [asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.8)); size(150);
pair B = (0,0), C = (6,0), A = (3,4), Q = (3.3,3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle^^B--Q^^C--Q^^A--Q);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,S); label("$Q$",Q,S);
[/asy]

How many different ways are there to select 5 representatives from 5 girls and 7 boys under the following conditions?

(1) At least one girl is selected;

(2) A specific boy (let's call him Boy A) and a specific girl (let's call her Girl B) are selected;

(3) At least one of Boy A or Girl B is selected.

The point $P$ is the center of a circle that circumscribes triangle $XYZ$, where $\angle XPY = 150^\circ$ and $\angle XPZ = 110^\circ$. Determine the degree measure of $\angle XYZ$.

[asy]
pair X,Y,Z;
draw(Circle((0,0),20),linewidth(0.7));
label("$P$",(0,0),S);
X=(-16,-12);
Z=(16,-12);
Y=(3,19.7);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
label("$110^\circ$",(-0.2,-0.3),W);
label("$150^\circ$",(0.2,0.4),E);
draw(Z--(0,0)--Y);
draw(X--(0,0));
label("$X$",X,SW);
label("$Y$",Y,NE);
label("$Z$",Z,SE);
[/asy]

In $\triangle ABC$, $AB=6$, $AC=3\sqrt{2}$, and $\overrightarrow{AB}\cdot \overrightarrow{AC}=-18$.  
$(1)$ Find the length of $BC$;  
$(2)$ Find the value of $\tan 2B$.

Given the following four propositions:  
① The negation of the proposition "For all $x \in \mathbb{R}, \cos x > 0$" is "There exists $x \in \mathbb{R}$ such that $\cos x \leq 0$";  
② If $0 < a < 1$, then the function $f(x) = x^2 + a^x - 3$ has only one zero;  
③ The function $y = \sin(2x - \frac{\pi}{3})$ has a monotonic increasing interval of $\left[- \frac{\pi}{12}, \frac{5\pi}{12}\right]$;  
④ For any real number $x$, if $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f′(x) > 0$ when $x > 0$, then $f′(x) < 0$ when $x < 0$.  
The numbers of the true propositions are __________ (Fill in the numbers of all true propositions).

A portion of the graph of $f(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ is shown below.

What is the value of $10a-5b+3c-2d$?

[asy]
import graph; size(7cm); real lsf=0.5; pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black; real xmin=-3.25,xmax=4.25,ymin=-9.25,ymax=4.25;

pen cqcqcq=rgb(0.75,0.75,0.75);

/*grid*/ pen gs=linewidth(0.7)+cqcqcq+linetype("2 2"); real gx=1,gy=1;
for(real i=ceil(xmin/gx)*gx;i<=floor(xmax/gx)*gx;i+=gx) draw((i,ymin)--(i,ymax),gs); for(real i=ceil(ymin/gy)*gy;i<=floor(ymax/gy)*gy;i+=gy) draw((xmin,i)--(xmax,i),gs);

Label laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);

xaxis("",xmin,xmax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true); yaxis("",ymin,ymax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true);
real f1(real x){return x*(x-1)*(x-2)/8;} draw(graph(f1,-3.25,4.25),linewidth(0.75));
clip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);

[/asy]

In acute triangle $\text{ABC}$, the sides opposite to angles $\text{A}$, $\text{B}$, and $\text{C}$ are $\text{a}$, $\text{b}$, and $\text{c}$ respectively. Given that $\sin \text{A} = \frac{3}{5}$ and $\tan (\text{A} - \text{B}) = -\frac{1}{2}$.

(1) Find the value of $\tan \text{B}$;

(2) If $\text{b} = 5$, find the value of $\text{c}$.

In a Go game competition between two players, A and B, it is agreed that the first to win 3 games will be declared the winner of the competition, and the competition will end. Assuming in a single game, the probability of A winning is 0.6, and the probability of B winning is 0.4, with the results of each game being independent of each other. It is known that in the first 2 games, A and B each won one game.  
(1) Find the probability of A winning the competition;  
(2) Find the probability that the competition ends after 5 games.

In the figure below, each side of the rhombus has length 5 centimeters.
[asy]
  import graph;
  unitsize(2.0cm);
  real w = sqrt(3);
  draw((w, 0) -- (0, 1) -- (-w, 0) -- (0, -1) -- cycle);
  filldraw(Circle((-0.5, 0), 0.8 / sqrt(pi)), gray);
  label(" $60^\circ$ ", (w - 0.1, 0), W);
[/asy]
The circle lies entirely within the rhombus.  The area of the circle is  $n$  square centimeters, where  $n$  is a positive integer.  Compute the number of possible values of  $n$ .

Given the function $f(x)=xe^x-ax$ (where $a\in \mathbb{R}$ is a constant, and $e$ is the base of the natural logarithm),

(I) When $f(x) > 0$, find the range of real numbers $x$;

(II) When $a=2$, find the minimum positive integer $k$ such that $f(x)+k > 0$.

A region $S$ in the complex plane is defined by \begin{align*}
S = \{x + iy: - 1\le x\le1, - 1\le y\le1\}.
\end{align*}
A complex number $z = x + iy$ is chosen uniformly at random from $S$. What is the probability that $\left(\frac12 + \frac12i\right)z$ is also in $S$?

In the diagram, $TUV$ is a straight line. What is the measure of $\angle TVW,$ in degrees? [asy]
pair T=(0,0);
pair U=(1.5,0);
pair V=(2.5,0);
pair W=(1,1);

draw(W--T--U--V--W--U);

label("$T$",T,S);
label("$U$",U,S);
label("$V$",V,S);
label("$W$",W,N);

label("$58^\circ$",T+(.15,.05),NE);
label("$75^\circ$",W-(.02,.15),S);
label("$42^\circ$",V+(-.35,.05),NW);
[/asy]

In a trapezium $ABCD$, side $AB$ is parallel to side $CD$. If the length of $AD$ is $52$ units, length of $BC$ is $20$ units, and the distance from $B$ to a point $P$ on $AD$ (extending if necessary) such that $CP = 35$ units, what is the length of segment $BP$?
```asy
import olympiad; import geometry; size(150); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair D = (0,0), C = (18,0), A = (0,10), B = (10,10);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,W);
pair P = intersectionpoint(A--D,B--C);
label("$P$",P,E);
```

A uniform cubic toy has the numbers $1$, $2$, $3$, $4$, $5$, $6$ written on its faces. The toy is thrown twice. Find:
1) The probability that the numbers on the upper faces are equal;
2) The probability that the sum of the numbers on the upper faces is less than $5$.

The sequence is defined by the relations \( a_{1} = 1 \),

\[ a_{2n} = \begin{cases} 
a_{n}, & \text{if } n \text{ is even}, \\
2a_{n}, & \text{if } n \text{ is odd}
\end{cases} \]

and 

\[ a_{2n+1} = \begin{cases} 
2a_{n}+1, & \text{if } n \text{ is even}, \\
a_{n}, & \text{if } n \text{ is odd}
\end{cases}. \]

Find the smallest natural number \( n \) for which \( a_{n} = a_{2017} \).

Consider the natural implementation of computing Fibonacci numbers:

\begin{tabular}{l}
1: \textbf{FUNCTION}  $\text{FIB}(n)$ : 
2: $\qquad$  \textbf{IF}  $n = 0$  \textbf{OR}  $n = 1$  \textbf{RETURN} 1 
3: $\qquad$  \textbf{RETURN}  $\text{FIB}(n-1) + \text{FIB}(n-2)$ \end{tabular}

When  $\text{FIB}(10)$  is evaluated, how many recursive calls to  $\text{FIB}$  occur?

Calculate:

(I) The value of $\dfrac{C_{5}^{2}+C_{5}^{3}}{A_{5}^{3}}$;

(II) The derivative $f'(x)$ of $f(x) = e^{-x}\sin 2x$.

In the diagram, the square has a perimeter of 64, and the triangle has a height of 32. If the square and the triangle have the same area, what is the value of $x$?
[asy]
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
draw((3,0)--(6,0)--(6,5)--cycle);
draw((5.8,0)--(5.8,.2)--(6,.2));
label("$x$",(4.5,0),S);
label("32",(6,2.5),E);
[/asy]

Consider the system of equations:
\begin{align*}
4x - 2y &= a, \\
9y - 18x &= b.
\end{align*}
If this system has a solution, find $\frac{a}{b}$, where $b \neq 0.$

In $\triangle PQR$, $PQ = PR$, $m\angle PRS = 50^{\circ}$, and $RS \parallel PQ$. What is the number of degrees in $m\angle QRS$?

[asy] pair P,Q,R,S,T; Q = dir(-50); P = dir(-130); S = (.6,0); T = .5 * dir(50);
draw(R--Q--P--T,EndArrow); draw(R--S,EndArrow);
label("$P$",P,W); label("$R$",R,NW);label("$Q$",Q,E);label("$S$",S,E);label("$T$",T,E);
[/asy]

The sequence $\{a\_n\}$ is a geometric sequence with the first term $a\_1=4$, and $S\_3$, $S\_2$, $S\_4$ form an arithmetic sequence.
(1) Find the general term formula of the sequence $\{a\_n\}$;
(2) If $b\_n=\log \_{2}|a\_n|$, let $T\_n$ be the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence $\{\frac{1}{b\_n b\_{n+1}}\}$. If $T\_n \leqslant \lambda b\_{n+1}$ holds for all $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$, find the minimum value of the real number $\lambda$.

Given the following four propositions:
1. There are infinitely many lines that can be drawn through a point outside a plane at an angle $\theta$ to the plane;
2. If a line is parallel to two intersecting planes, then it must also be parallel to their line of intersection;
3. For two given skew lines, there is exactly one plane through any point in space that is parallel to both of these skew lines;
4. For two skew lines, there are infinitely many planes that form the same angle with these two skew lines.

The correct propositions are     . (Please fill in the numbers of all correct propositions)

In the diagram, what is the measure of $\angle ACB$ in degrees? [asy]
size(250);
draw((-60,0)--(0,0));
draw((0,0)--(64.3,76.6)--(166,0)--cycle);
label("$A$",(64.3,76.6),N);
label("$105^\circ$",(64.3,73),S);
label("$120^\circ$",(0,0),NW);
label("$B$",(0,0),S);
label("$D$",(-60,0),S);
label("$C$",(166,0),S);
[/asy]

Given that the function $f(x)$ is an odd function on the interval $(-\infty, +\infty)$, and the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric about the line $x=1$. For $x\in [0,1]$, we have $f(x)=2^x-1$.  
(1) Determine the explicit formula for $f(x)$ when $x\in [1,2]$;  
(2) Calculate the value of $f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+...+f(2016)$.

How many continuous paths from $A$ to $B$, along segments of the figure, do not revisit any of the seven labeled points after adding a point $G$ on the segment $DF$?

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,2)--(0,2)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(2));
draw((0,2)--(1,0)--(3,2)--(0,2)--cycle,linewidth(2));
draw((0,2)--(1.5,3.5)--(3,2),linewidth(2));
draw((1,0)--(2,1),linewidth(2)); // Adding a new point G

label("$A$",(1.5,3.5),N);
label("$B$",(0,0),SW);
label("$C$",(0,2),W);
label("$D$",(3,2),E);
label("$E$",(3,0),SE);
label("$F$",(1,0),S);
label("$G$",(2,1),NE); // New point G
[/asy]

Given that the graph of a linear function $y=kx+b$ passes through the point (-1, -5) and intersects the graph of the proportional function $y=\frac{1}{2}x$ at the point (2, a), find:
1. The value of a;
2. The values of k and b;
3. The area of the triangle formed by the graphs of these two functions and the x-axis.

Unit square  $ABCD$  is divided into four rectangles by  $EF$  and  $GH$ , with  $BF = \frac14$ .  $EF$  is parallel to  $AB$  and  $GH$  parallel to  $BC$ .  $EF$  and  $GH$  meet at point  $P$ . Suppose  $BF + DH = FH$ , calculate the nearest integer to the degree of  $\angle FAH$ .
[asy]
size(100); defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10)); 
pair D2(pair P) {
dot(P,linewidth(3)); return P;
}
// NOTE: I've tampered with the angles to make the diagram not-to-scale. The correct numbers should be 72 instead of 76, and 45 instead of 55. 
pair A=(0,1), B=(0,0), C=(1,0), D=(1,1), F=intersectionpoints(A--A+2*dir(-76),B--C)[0], H=intersectionpoints(A--A+2*dir(-76+55),D--C)[0], E=F+(0,1), G=H-(1,0), P=intersectionpoints(E--F,G--H)[0];
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(F--A--H); draw(E--F); draw(G--H); 
label(" $A$ ",D2(A),NW);
label(" $B$ ",D2(B),SW);
label(" $C$ ",D2(C),SE);
label(" $D$ ",D2(D),NE);
label(" $E$ ",D2(E),plain.N);
label(" $F$ ",D2(F),S);
label(" $G$ ",D2(G),W);
label(" $H$ ",D2(H),plain.E);
label(" $P$ ",D2(P),SE);
[/asy]

Given the function $f(x) = 4\sin(2x + \frac{\pi}{3})$, $(x \in \mathbb{R})$, determine the correct proposition(s) from the following:

1. $y = f(x)$ is a periodic function with the smallest positive period of $2\pi$;
2. The graph of $y = f(x)$ is symmetrical about the point $(-\frac{\pi}{6}, 0)$;
3. The graph of $y = f(x)$ is symmetrical about the line $x = -\frac{5\pi}{12}$.

Each outcome on the spinner below has equal probability. If you spin the spinner four times and form a four-digit number from the four outcomes, such that the first outcome is the thousands digit, the second outcome is the hundreds digit, the third outcome is the tens digit, and the fourth outcome is the units digit, what is the probability that you will end up with a four-digit number that is divisible by 5? Express your answer as a common fraction.

[asy]
draw(Circle((0,0),10));
draw((0,0)--(8.7,-5));
draw((0,0)--(-8.7,-5));
draw((0,0)--(0,10));
label("1",(7,7),SW);
label("3",(-7,7),SE);
label("2",(0,-2),S);
draw((0,0)--(3,5),Arrow);
[/asy]

In $\triangle ABC$, $a$, $b$, and $c$ are the sides opposite to angles $A$, $B$, and $C$ respectively. Given that $\frac{\cos C}{\cos B} = \frac{2a - c}{b}$ and $a + c = 2$.
1. Find angle $B$;
2. Find the minimum value of the length of side $b$.

A rubber band is wrapped around two pipes as shown. One has radius  $3$  inches and the other has radius  $9$  inches. The length of the band can be expressed as  $a\pi+b\sqrt{c}$  where  $a$ ,  $b$ ,  $c$  are integers and  $c$  is square free. What is  $a+b+c$ ?

[asy]
size(4cm);
draw(circle((0,0),3));
draw(circle((12,0),9));
draw(3*dir(120)--(12,0)+9*dir(120));
draw(3*dir(240)--(12,0)+9*dir(240));
[/asy]

The smaller square in the figure has a perimeter of $8$ cm, and the larger square has an area of $81$ $\text{cm}^2$. Determine the distance from point $C$ to point $D$. Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest tenth.

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(18,0));
draw((4,0)--(4,14));
draw((0,0)--(0,4));
draw((0,4)--(4,4));
draw((0,4)--(18,14));
draw((18,0)--(18,14));
draw((4,14)--(18,14));
label("D",(0,4),W);
label("C",(18,14),E);
[/asy]

In the figure below, if the area of $\triangle ABC$ is 50, what is the value of $p$? [asy]
size(5cm);defaultpen(fontsize(9));
pair o = (0, 0); pair q = (0, 15); pair b = (15, 0);
pair a = (3, 15); pair t = (3, 0); pair c = (0, p);

draw((-2, 0)--(18, 0), Arrow);
draw((0, -2)--(0, 18), Arrow);
draw(q--a--b);
draw(a--c--b);

label("$Q(0, 15)$", q, W);
label("$A(3, 15)$", a, NE);
label("$B(15, 0)$", b, S);
label("$O(0, 0)$", o, SW);
label("$x$", (18, 0), E);
label("$y$", (0, 18), N);
label("$C(0, p)$", c, W);
[/asy]

Given that the vertex of angle \\(\alpha\\) coincides with the origin \\(O\\), its initial side coincides with the non-negative half-axis of the \\(x\\)-axis, and its terminal side passes through point \\(P(- \dfrac {3}{5},- \dfrac {4}{5}).\\)  
\\((\\)I\\()\\) Find the value of \\(\sin (\alpha+\pi)\\);  
\\((\\)II\\()\\) If angle \\(\beta\\) satisfies \\(\sin (\alpha+\beta)= \dfrac {5}{13}\\), find the value of \\(\cos \beta\\).

The figure shown is a cube. The distance between vertices $A$ and $G$ of the cube is $5\sqrt{3}$ units. What is the volume of the cube, in cubic units?

[asy]
size(3cm,3cm);
pair A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H;

A=(0,0);
D=(1,0);
C=(1.5,0.5);
B=(0.5,0.5);
E=(0,1);
H=(1,1);
G=(1.5,1.5);
F=(0.5,1.5);

draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(E--F--G--H--cycle);
draw(A--E);
draw(B--F);
draw(C--G);
draw(D--H);

label("$A$", A, W);
label("$G$", G, NE);

dot(A);
dot(G);
[/asy]

Calculate:

1. $40 + \left(\frac{1}{6} - \frac{2}{3} + \frac{3}{4}\right) \times 12$;
2. $\left(-1\right)^{2021} + |-9| \times \frac{2}{3} + \left(-3\right) \div \frac{1}{5}$.

Given the polar equation of curve $C$ is $\rho =1$, and the parametric equation of the line is $\begin{cases} & x=1+\dfrac{t}{2} \\ & y=2+\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}t \end{cases}$ ($t$ is the parameter).

$(1)$ Write the Cartesian coordinate equations of the line and curve $C$;

$(2)$ Suppose curve $C$ undergoes a scaling transformation $\begin{cases} & x'=2x \\ & y'=y \end{cases}$ to obtain curve $C'$, and let any point on curve $C'$ be $M(x,y)$, find the minimum value of $x+2\sqrt{3}y$.

Define a function \( g(n) \) as follows:
\[
g(n) = 
\begin{cases}
3n^2 + 3 & \text{if } n \text{ is odd} \\
\frac{n}{3} & \text{if } n \text{ is divisible by } 3 \\
n-1 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
For how many integers \( n \) from 1 to 100, inclusive, does \( g(g(\ldots g(n) \ldots)) = 1 \) for some number of applications of \( g \)?

Given $a+b=4$ and $ab=1$, find:
(1) The value of $a^2+b^2$;
(2) The value of $(a-b)^2$.

Given the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}+ \frac{nx}{mx+n}$.

(1) If $m=0$, $n=1$, find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$;

(2) If $m > 0$, $n > 0$, and the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,+\infty)$ is $1$, find the maximum value of $\frac{m}{n}$.

A bridge is constructed by placing a wooden plank on top of two triangular supports made of metal, as shown in the diagram [asy]:
import olympiad;
import math;

// Draw triangles
pair A = (0, 1);
pair B = (-cos(1.3962), 0);
pair C = (cos(1.3962), 0);
pair D = (2, 1);
pair E = (2-cos(1.3962), 0);  // Modified this for aesthetics but math same as original
pair F = (2+cos(1.3962), 0);  // Modified this for aesthetics but math same as original
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(D--E--F--cycle);
draw(A--D);
label('$A$',A,N);
label('$B$',B,S);
label('$C$',C,S);
label('$D$',D,N);
label('$E$',E,S);
label('$F$',F,S);
[/asy] Given $AB = AC$ and $DE = DF,$ and assuming $\angle BAC = 15^\circ$ and $\angle EDF = 25^\circ,$ determine $\angle DAC + \angle ADE$.

Find $AX$ in the diagram where $CX$ bisects $\angle ACB$.

[asy]
import markers;

real t=.64;
pair A=(0,0);
pair B=(4,3);
pair C=(1,2);
pair X=t*A+(1-t)*B;

draw(C--A--B--C--X);

label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$X$",X,SE);

label("$34$",.5*(B+X),SE);
label("$40$",.5*(B+C),N);
label("$25$",.5*(A+C),NW);

[/asy]

Given vectors $\overrightarrow{a}=(\sin x,1)$ and $\overrightarrow{b}=(\cos x,2)$, where $x\in \mathbb{R}$, and the function $f(x)=\overrightarrow{a}\cdot \overrightarrow{b}$.

$(1)$ When $x\in \left[ -\frac{\pi}{12},\frac{\pi}{3} \right]$, find the maximum and minimum values of $|\overrightarrow{a}+\overrightarrow{b}|$;

$(2)$ If $f(\alpha)=\frac{12}{5}$, where $\alpha \in \left( \frac{\pi}{4},\frac{\pi}{2} \right)$, find $\tan \left( 2\alpha +\frac{3\pi}{4} \right)$

Let \[f(x) =
\begin{cases}
3x^2 - 4&\text{if } x\le 3, \\
bx + 5 &\text{if } x>3.
\end{cases}
\] Find $b$ if the graph of $y=f(x)$ is continuous.

Let \[f(n) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
n^2-4 & \text{ if }n<0, \\
2n-24 & \text{ if }n \geq 0.
\end{array}
\right.\]What is the positive difference between the two values of \( a \) that satisfy the equation \( f(-1)+f(4)+f(a)=0 \)?

An acute isosceles triangle, $ABC$, is inscribed in a circle. Through $B$ and $C$, tangents to the circle are drawn, meeting at point $D$. If $\angle ABC = \angle ACB = 3 (\angle D$) and $\angle BAC = t \pi$ in radians, then find $t$.

[asy]
import graph;

unitsize(2 cm);

pair O, A, B, C, D;

